// Data model: theorems of set theory and their proofs, from the axioms of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with choice to
// the number systems, ordinals and cardinals, the axiom of choice and its equivalents, ordered sets, inner models
// (Gödel's constructible universe) and forcing (Cohen). The axioms of ZF and the axiom of choice are nodes of kind
// "axiom"; they are the premises from which the other theorems are proved.
//
// Model (see reports/2026-11-03 Satzgraphen aller Gebiete/auftrag.md): theorems id, name, kind, area, field, msc, msc5,
// premises, formula, text, proof, concepts, year, who.
//   `premises`: the theorems that one usual textbook proof (named in `proof`) actually uses; only direct premises.
//   `area`: as in the concept file data/concepts.js (five-character class for 03E, three-character class for 06A and
//           06B, the grouping "numbers" for the construction of the number systems, "basic" for other areas).
//   `concepts`: concepts of data/concepts.js that occur in the statement.
//   `proofName`, `proofYear`, `proofWho`: the standard proof given by `premises` and `proof`; `alternativeProofs`:
//           further proofs { name, premises, idea, year, who }, most important first; `foundation: true`: general tool
//           of the area whose premises the map does not expand (reports/2026-11-03 Satzgraphen Beweise ueberarbeiten).
//
// Prefix for references from other folders: "st:<id>".
// Sources: Enderton, Elements of Set Theory (1977); Halmos, Naive Set Theory (1960); Jech, Set Theory (3rd ed. 2003);
// Kunen, Set Theory (1980 and 2011); Landau, Grundlagen der Analysis (1930). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory. tests/check-theorems.js warns about theorems without
// premises that have neither mark.

