      proofName: "Dedekind's intersection of closed relations", proofYear: 1888, proofWho: "Dedekind",
      alternativeProofs: [
        { name: "Union of approximations", premises: ["induction-principle", "cartesian-product-exists", "ax-union"],
          idea: "Induction shows that for every n there is exactly one function on {0, …, n} satisfying the recursion and that any two such functions agree where both are defined; f is the union of all of them." },
      ],
      formula: String.raw`f(0) = a,\quad f(n + 1) = g(f(n))`,
      text: "For every set A, every a ∈ A and every map g : A → A there is exactly one map f : ℕ → A with f(0) = a and f(n + 1) = g(f(n)) for all n.",
